    "description": "Mine safety combines engineering controls, monitoring, work organization, and emergency preparedness. The Tabas coal-mine disaster illustrates the importance of distinguishing documented events from verified findings about their causes and the effectiveness of subsequent reforms.",
    "tags": ["Mine safety", "Coal mining", "Tabas", "Methane", "Occupational health"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:23:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:23:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mine Safety in Iran: Hazards, Prevention and Evidence",
      "description": "Understand mine-safety principles, the documented Tabas disaster, and the evidence needed to assess protection and enforcement in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "tabas-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why Tabas matters",
        "content": "A methane-related explosion struck a coal mine at Tabas in South Khorasan Province on 21 September 2024. Associated Press reporting described a major loss of life and noted that the safety procedures in place were initially unclear. The event establishes the gravity of the hazard, but early news reports do not by themselves establish the complete technical chain of failure or legal responsibility. [s1]\n\nFor international readers, the distinction matters: an accident report, a survivor's account, and a completed engineering investigation answer different questions. This page does not treat initial reporting as a final investigation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "methane-control",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the methane hazard",
        "content": "Methane control is a specialized engineering task. The US National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health, a research agency known as NIOSH, identifies underground accumulation of explosive methane as a major coal-mining hazard. Ventilation is designed to dilute and control those accumulations; its research also addresses predicting emissions and evaluating methane drainage. [s2]\n\nThat explains why 'the mine has ventilation' is an incomplete description of protection. The relevant question is whether the system controls gas where mining is occurring and as conditions change. Ventilation and methane drainage address related aspects of the hazard, but their suitability depends on the mine. These international engineering principles help explain the Iranian accident context; they are not evidence that any particular Iranian mine installed, maintained, or operated the relevant controls. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "beyond-explosion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Safety includes routine work and long-term exposure",
        "content": "The International Labour Organization's underground-coalmining code covers a much wider field than explosions: respirable dust, physical and chemical hazards, transport, equipment, and emergency arrangements. It also addresses reporting, occupational-health services, and the responsibilities of employers, workers, contractors, and public authorities. The code is guidance, expressly distinguished from binding national legislation. [s3]\n\nWorker participation is part of that framework. The code calls for workers to be informed about hazards, report dangerous conditions, request investigations, and remove themselves from reasonably perceived serious danger without retaliation. These are the code's recommended protections; their inclusion here does not establish their implementation or enforceability in Iran. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "emergency-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Preparedness and accountability",
        "content": "ILO Recommendation No. 183 connects underground ventilation with reducing explosion risk and maintaining an appropriate working atmosphere. It also identifies hazards requiring operating plans, including fires, gas outbursts, water inflows, and rockfalls. The point is that preparedness must match the mechanism of danger rather than rely on a single general emergency plan. This recommendation is an international reference, not a statement of current Iranian legal requirements. [s4]\n\nA credible assessment of improvement after Tabas would therefore need both technical and institutional evidence: the final investigation, the applicable Iranian rules, inspection findings, documented corrective actions, and subsequent monitoring. Those materials were not verified in this research. The available evidence supports explaining prevention principles and the documented disaster, but it does not support declaring that the underlying problems have been resolved. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
