    "page_path": "/explore/mining/mining-technology/valuable-material-recovery/",
    "name": "Valuable Material Recovery",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/mining/mining-technology/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian research examines the recovery of useful minerals remaining in iron-ore and base-metal tailings. These studies show why residual streams require individual characterization and why successful laboratory separation does not establish commercial viability.",
    "tags": ["Tailings", "Material recovery", "Zinc", "Copper", "Molybdenum", "Resource efficiency"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:23:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:23:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Valuable Material Recovery in Iran: Learning from Tailings",
      "description": "Explore Iranian studies of zinc, copper, molybdenum, and iron recovery from tailings, with clear distinctions between experiments and operating plants."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "residual-resource",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains after processing",
        "content": "Tailings are residual materials from mineral concentration, but their designation as a waste stream does not mean that all useful minerals have been removed. Iranian research has investigated residual iron, zinc-bearing minerals, copper, and molybdenum. These materials offer different separation problems rather than a single interchangeable secondary resource. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nA useful starting question is therefore not simply whether a tailings deposit contains metal. It is whether the metal occurs in minerals that a suitable process can separate. Characterization and separation tests are necessary steps toward answering that question; they do not establish that commercial recovery is already taking place. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iron-streams",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovering selected streams rather than treating everything alike",
        "content": "An Iranian iron-ore study compared individual tailings streams with their combined output. The researchers measured composition, particle size, mineralogy, and magnetic behavior before testing wet magnetic separation. Different streams contained different dominant iron minerals, and the selected rougher-stage material responded better than the combined tailings. [s1]\n\nThe practical implication is specific: combining residual streams can conceal differences relevant to recovery. A promising result for one stream cannot automatically be applied to the entire storage facility. The published abstract supports this comparison, but it does not establish a national recovery rate or the economics of a full-scale project. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zinc-recovery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Kooshk: the behavior of zinc-bearing minerals",
        "content": "Research associated with Kooshk mine examined old tailings containing sphalerite, a zinc-bearing sulfide mineral, alongside pyrite, an iron sulfide. The investigators tested flotation reagents and the effects of pretreating the slurry by scrubbing or ultrasound. They also examined how changing pH affected slurry viscosity and selective separation. [s2]\n\nThe experiments found that reagent combinations and pretreatment could improve sphalerite flotation. Increasing alkalinity in the tested conditions also raised viscosity and reduced separation performance. These results illustrate why a recovery process requires attention to the behavior of the entire slurry, rather than metal content alone. [s2]\n\nThe abstract describes experimental performance. It does not demonstrate continuous commercial operation, establish present-day production at Kooshk, or provide a complete accounting of treatment costs and residual-waste management. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "copper-molybdenum",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sarcheshmeh: testing fresh tailings",
        "content": "A separate study investigated copper and molybdenum recovery from fresh final tailings at Sarcheshmeh. Its laboratory program examined classification, grinding, flotation reagents, and alternative circuit arrangements. The research objective was simultaneous recovery from a low-grade residual feed. [s3]\n\nFresh tailings are a defined feed in this investigation. Results should not be transferred without testing to older stored material or another plant's residues. Equally, a successful laboratory circuit is evidence of separation potential, not proof of an operating industrial project. The inspected abstract supports the research approach and laboratory scale; it does not establish commercial deployment. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recovery-workflow",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How the cited studies investigate recovery potential",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Sample a defined tailings stream\"] -->|\"Establish feed properties\"| B[\"Characterize composition and minerals\"]\n  B -->|\"Select candidate separation methods\"| C[\"Run controlled separation tests\"]\n  C -->|\"Vary relevant conditions\"| D[\"Compare process responses\"]\n  D -->|\"Assess experimental evidence\"| E[\"Identify recovery potential and limits\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This synthesis shows the experimental logic shared by the cited studies. It ends with an assessment of recovery potential and does not imply commercial deployment.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
